# http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Class
# 
# http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept
<http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
# 
# http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme
<http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AIAgent
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AIAgent> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AIAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AIAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An autonomous or semi-autonomous system driven by AI/ML that can act, make decisions, or be attributed responsibility in a cyber or sociotechnical context." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AIAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "AI Agent" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AIAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers both offensive uses (AI-assisted or autonomous threat actors) and defensive uses (AI-driven detection, response, or hunting systems). Distinct from Tool — an AIAgent has a degree of autonomous decision-making rather than being a passively invoked artifact."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AITechnique
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AITechnique> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AITechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AITechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specific method by which adversaries attack AI/ML systems (e.g., training data poisoning, adversarial examples, model extraction)."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AITechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "AI Technique"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccessControlList
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccessControlList> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccessControlList> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccessControlList> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured list of access control entries (ACEs) that governs which principals (users, groups, or system accounts) are permitted or denied specific operations on a resource."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccessControlList> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Access Control List"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccessControlList> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applies across filesystems, operating system objects (registry keys, processes), network devices (firewall rules), cloud IAM policies, and APIs. In a CTI context, adversaries frequently modify ACLs to establish persistence, escalate privileges, or deny defenders access to compromised resources. Framework-specific extensions (e.g. Windows DACL/SACL structure) are modeled at the framework layer."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A named account held by an Agent with a provider — an authenticated-access identity, a value-holding ledger position, or both aspects together — identified by one or more Identifiers and optionally attributed to a known Agent."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Account"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Abstract parent of stonework:UserAccount (authenticated access) and stonework:FinancialAccount (value custody and movement). It exists chiefly to give stonework:hasAccount and stonework:accountOf a range spanning both without widening to every DigitalIdentity. A single real-world account often has both natures; model them as two individuals linked by stonework:controls rather than one individual of both types, since one login commonly governs several ledgers."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccountType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccountType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid257 .
_:genid257 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#accountTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#accountTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid257 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
<http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
_:genid257 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a user account by its authentication or deployment context."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Account Type"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Actuator
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Actuator> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Actuator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhysicalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Actuator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A physical device that effects a change in a physical process or plant — for example a valve, motor, relay, or servo — as distinct from a cyber security safeguard mechanism."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Actuator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Actuator"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Actuator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use for ICS and OT field devices that act on a physical process. Distinct from stonework:SecurityActuator, which is deployed infrastructure that applies a countermeasure. A PLC or motor starter is an Actuator; an EDR agent or firewall is a SecurityActuator. An ICS device that also runs processes and participates in networks may additionally be typed as stonework:Host."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Any entity that can act, perform actions, or be attributed responsibility in a cyber or sociotechnical context."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Agent" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers AI, human individuals, groups, organizations, and other actors (e.g. threat actors, vendors, government bodies). Used as the top-level superclass for identity-related objects in alignment with STIX Identity."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Analysis
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Analysis> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Analysis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Analysis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A systematic examination of a cyber entity, artifact, or event that produces structured findings about its nature, behavior, or properties."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Analysis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Analysis"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Analysis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Sibling to stonework:Assessment under IntelligenceProduct. Where Assessment answers 'how bad/likely/risky?', Analysis answers 'what is it and what does it do?' Superclass for domain-specific analyses such as MalwareAnalysis. Aligns with STIX MalwareAnalysis SDO at the subclass level."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AnalyticTechnique
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AnalyticTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AnalyticTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AnalyticTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A prescribed analytical method used to process, evaluate, or reason over data to produce intelligence, detections, or assessments."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AnalyticTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Analytic Technique" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AnalyticTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers structured analytic techniques (e.g., analysis of competing hypotheses, kill chain analysis, diamond model analysis) and computational analytic methods (e.g., clustering, anomaly detection). Distinct from DetectionRule — an AnalyticTechnique describes the method; a DetectionRule is a deployable artifact implementing it."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured knowledge artifact capturing the evaluation, analysis, or estimation of risk, likelihood, impact, or other security-relevant attributes."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Assessment" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers risk assessments, vulnerability assessments, and impact analyses. Assessment is an Information artifact — the output of an evaluative process — not the process itself. The process producing an Assessment may be modeled as a stonework:Process or stonework:Investigation."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackPattern
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackPattern> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackPattern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackPattern> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A type of Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTP) that describes ways that adversaries attempt to compromise targets. Often maps to MITRE ATT&CK techniques."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackPattern> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Attack Pattern"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackPattern> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The abstract concept spanning framework-specific shapes that are not structurally identical: CAPEC's shape (capec:AttackPattern) is a taxonomy entry with severity/likelihood/prerequisite fields; MITRE ATT&CK's shape (stonework:AttackTechnique) is an operationalized technique with sub-techniques, procedures, mitigations, and detections. Both are AttackPatterns; stonework:relatedAttackPattern links instances across shapes that describe the same underlying technique."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackTechnique
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackPattern> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A prescribed offensive method used by an adversary to achieve a specific tactical objective within a target environment."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Attack Technique"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AttackTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Aligns with MITRE ATT&CK techniques and D3FEND OffensiveTechnique. The MITRE ATT&CK-shaped realization of stonework:AttackPattern — operationalized for execution, as distinct from CAPEC's more abstract taxonomy-entry shape (capec:AttackPattern, also a stonework:AttackPattern). Framework-specific subclasses (e.g. attack:Technique) provide the concrete individuals. A specific, concrete realization of this technique is modeled as a stonework:Procedure, linked back via stonework:implementedBy."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AutonomousSystem
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AutonomousSystem> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AutonomousSystem> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AutonomousSystem> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents an Autonomous System (AS), a collection of IP routing prefixes under the control of a single administrative entity, identified by its AS number." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#AutonomousSystem> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Autonomous System"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BankAccountNumber
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BankAccountNumber> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BankAccountNumber> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BankAccountNumber> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A domestic bank account number as issued under a national scheme, without IBAN normalization."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BankAccountNumber> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Bank Account Number"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BankAccountNumber> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The value is stonework:accountNumber. The routing context (US ABA routing number, UK sort code) is recorded on the holding stonework:FinancialInstitution via stonework:abaRoutingNumber or an equivalent, not on this identifier."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A pattern of actions or methods characteristic of an entity, particularly adversaries."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Behavior" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Groups TTP-related classes: Tactic, Technique (and subclasses), and Procedure. In BFO terms, Behaviors are GenericallyDependentContinuants — specifications or patterns describing how actions are carried out. The execution of a Technique is a CyberActivity; the Technique itself is a Behavior. Aligns with IAO PlanSpecification."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComponentContext> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid258 .
_:genid258 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onClass> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> .
_:genid258 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#maxQualifiedCardinality> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger> .
_:genid258 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#installationOf> .
_:genid258 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "BomEntry is the STONEWORK representation of a CycloneDX component element or SPDX package entry. It links a VersionedProduct into the context of a particular SBOM document via stonework:installationOf, and may carry component-specific metadata (PURL, license, hash, component type) that does not belong on the VersionedProduct itself."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A single component entry within a Software Bill of Materials, asserting that a specific VersionedProduct is included in the artifact or system described by the SBOM."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "BOM Entry" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#BomEntry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "An SBOM import creates one BomEntry per declared component. The BomEntry IRI is scoped to the SBOM (e.g. <sbom-iri/entry/<component-ref>>). Multiple BomEntry instances across different SBOMs may reference the same VersionedProduct via stonework:installationOf."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Campaign
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Campaign> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Campaign> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Campaign> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A grouping of adversary behaviours and resources with a common objective pursued over a period of time. Campaigns may be attributed to ThreatActors and may form part of a broader CyberActivityCluster." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Campaign> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Campaign"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Campaign> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A Campaign is organized around an objective and time period, while a CyberActivityCluster is organized by analytic correlation. A Campaign that is one episode within a broader activity cluster is linked to it with stonework:memberOfActivityCluster. The same body of activity may be typed as both classes when both descriptions apply."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid259 .
_:genid259 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#categoryScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#categoryScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid259 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid259 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification or tagging concept used to group or qualify other individuals in a particular context."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Category"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Categories are reified so they can be referenced, versioned, or scoped. They are not rigid types."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Certificate
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Certificate> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Certificate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Certificate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured digital document binding a public key to an identity, issued and signed by a certificate authority. Used for authentication, encryption, and trust establishment in networked systems."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Certificate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Certificate"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Certificate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Primarily represents X.509 certificates. May be used to cluster infrastructure sharing the same certificate or to link observed TLS/SSL artifacts to their issuing authority. Referenced by stonework:sharesCertificate on Infrastructure. Represent certificate fingerprints through stonework:hasHash and a stonework:Hash individual rather than algorithm-specific fingerprint literals."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#City
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#City> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#City> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#City> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A significant urban settlement, municipality, or metropolitan area." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#City> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "City" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComparisonOperator
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComparisonOperator> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComparisonOperator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A controlled vocabulary of operators used to compare a Variable's bound value against a threshold in a Transition guard."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComparisonOperator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Comparison Operator" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComparisonOperator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Individuals are stonework:Equals, stonework:NotEquals, stonework:LessThan, stonework:LessThanOrEqual, stonework:GreaterThan, and stonework:GreaterThanOrEqual. Referenced by stonework:guardOperator; omitting an operator on a guard defaults to Equals semantics."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComponentContext
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComponentContext> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComponentContext> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An abstract class representing a context-specific reference to a VersionedProduct: the same product version appears differently depending on whether it is declared in a bill of materials or observed as a running installation on a specific system."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComponentContext> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Component Context" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComponentContext> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Subclassed by stonework:BomEntry (declarative — listed in an SBOM document), stonework:ProductInstallation (observational — verified deployment via SWID tag or scan), and framework-specific subclasses such as cve:AffectedProduct (advisory — named in a source vulnerability record, possibly against several VersionedProduct individuals at once). Unifies these evidence bases under a single superclass so that stonework:installationOf can span all of them without domain restrictions forcing incorrect inferences. BomEntry and ProductInstallation are additionally restricted to at most one installationOf value each; ComponentContext itself imposes no such limit, since not every subclass shares that one-to-one shape."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Control may represent individual controls, control enhancements, or sub‑controls. Framework‑specific identifiers (e.g., \"AC‑2\", \"CIS 4.1\") SHOULD be modeled using explicit properties rather than encoded in the class IRI. Control text SHOULD be referenced via citations rather than reproduced verbatim." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A prescribed security or privacy safeguard that specifies an action, capability, or condition intended to reduce risk, enforce policy, or achieve a defined protection objective within an information system or organization." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Control" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Control> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "This class provides a semantic representation of security and privacy controls from frameworks such as NIST SP 800‑53 and the CIS Controls. It supports mapping to external control identifiers and families without embedding copyrighted control text." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ControlPlan
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ControlPlan> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ControlPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ControlPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A concrete, Step-decomposed sequence of actions that implements a Control in a specific operational context."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ControlPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Control Plan" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ControlPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Mirrors the Technique/Procedure split on the defensive side: stonework:Control is the abstract prescribed safeguard (a catalog entry, e.g. NIST AC-2 or a CIS control); ControlPlan is its concrete implementation as a Step sequence, linked back via stonework:implements. A ControlPlan is typically invoked from within a Playbook or DetectionPlan by a Step that stonework:implements it."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Any control, mitigation, or safeguard applied to protect against, reduce, or respond to security risks."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Countermeasure" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes Controls, Mitigations, CoursesOfAction, and DetectionRules. The two primary branches are preventive/reactive measures (Mitigation, CourseOfAction, Control) and detection logic (DetectionRule, Signature). A Countermeasure describes the safeguard or procedure; the deployed mechanism that can apply it is a stonework:SecurityActuator, and a particular application is a stonework:SecurityActuation."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Country
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Country> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Country> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Country> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A sovereign state or top-level administrative entity recognized internationally." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Country> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Country" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CourseOfAction
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CourseOfAction> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CourseOfAction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CourseOfAction> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specific, contextualized recommended action or set of steps to prevent or respond to a threat, attack, or incident."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CourseOfAction> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Course of Action"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CourseOfAction> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "More prescriptive and situational than a general Mitigation — a CourseOfAction packages guidance for a specific threat context. Corresponds to the STIX 2.1 CourseOfAction SDO. General reusable controls (e.g. ATT&CK mitigations) are modeled as Mitigation; specific incident response actions are CourseOfAction."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAddress
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAddress> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An on-chain address that identifies a destination for value on a distributed ledger."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Crypto Address"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The address string is stonework:walletAddress. Attach a CryptoAddress to the wallet it identifies, a stonework:FinancialAccount, with stonework:hasIdentifier; the address's asset and network are then traversable through ^stonework:hasIdentifier/stonework:accountCurrency and stonework:blockchainNetwork. Sibling of stonework:EmailAddress and stonework:MACAddress."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAsset
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAsset> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Currency> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "virtual asset"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A cryptocurrency, stablecoin, or other token that serves as a unit of value on a distributed ledger."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Crypto Asset"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CryptoAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Instances are the assets themselves (e.g. Bitcoin, Ether, a specific ERC-20 token) — not wallets, addresses, or holdings. A wallet is modeled as a stonework:FinancialAccount and its address as a stonework:CryptoAddress; an amount of a crypto asset is a stonework:MonetaryAmount whose stonework:currency is the CryptoAsset. The same nominal token issued on multiple ledgers is modeled as distinct individuals distinguished by stonework:blockchainNetwork. 'Virtual asset' is the FATF term; a service that custodies or exchanges them is a virtual asset service provider (stonework:FinancialInstitution categorized accordingly)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Currency
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Currency> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Currency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Currency> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A unit in which monetary value is denominated — a fiat currency or a crypto asset."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Currency> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Currency"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Currency> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The range of stonework:currency on stonework:MonetaryAmount. Fiat units are supplied as a closed controlled vocabulary in stonework:fiatCurrencyScheme (ISO 4217). Crypto units are stonework:CryptoAsset individuals, created as needed rather than pre-enumerated. Currency itself carries no instances directly."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CvssMetric
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CvssMetric> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CvssMetric> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CvssMetric> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured technical-severity assessment of a vulnerability using the Common Vulnerability Scoring System, carrying a version, vector, base score, and qualitative severity."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CvssMetric> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "CVSS Metric"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CvssMetric> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use stonework:hasCvssMetric to attach one or more source-specific assessments to a Vulnerability. Identify the scoring authority with stonework:createdBy and preserve the source record with stonework:hasProvenance. The direct stonework:cvssBaseScore, stonework:cvssSeverity, and stonework:cvssVector properties are query-friendly headline projections. CVSS base metrics are context-neutral; environmental metrics may adjust technical severity for a deployment context, but CVSS does not establish whether the vulnerable product is present or constitute a full enterprise risk assessment. Use stonework:VulnerabilityAssessment for that contextual judgment. A plan that calculates or consumes a CVSS assessment may bind a Variable to this assessment, but the Variable is an execution-time value and does not replace the vulnerability-to-metric relationship."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specific occurrence or performance of actions by one or more agents over time within a cyber or sociotechnical context."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Cyber Activity" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "One of two root classes in STONEWORK alongside CyberEntity. Superclass for Campaigns, CyberActivityClusters, Incidents, Operations, Events, and Processes. Aligns with BFO Occurrent and BFO Process."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivityCluster
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivityCluster> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivityCluster> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivityCluster> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An analytically identified aggregate of cyber activities grouped because available evidence suggests a common source, mechanism, or operational thread."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivityCluster> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Cyber Activity Cluster"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivityCluster> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A CyberActivityCluster is unified by analytic correlation rather than the objective and time period of a Campaign, the unified command or intent of an Operation, the execution sequence of a Process, or the inquiry purpose of an Investigation. It does not require a known objective, responsible Agent, precise boundaries, or a conclusion that the activity is adversarial. It denotes the body of activity believed to be related, not the intelligence artifact recording that judgment; use stonework:Grouping, stonework:Report, or stonework:Assessment for the artifact. Only CyberActivity instances are members through stonework:memberOfActivityCluster and stonework:hasMemberActivity; relate tools, malware, infrastructure, and responsible participants through their specific relationships. The STIX adapter maps its narrower intrusion-set source class to CyberActivityCluster."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Any concept, object, or resource that exists or operates within a cyber or sociotechnical domain."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Cyber Entity" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "One of two root classes in STONEWORK. Groups all persistent entities — Agents, Infrastructure, DigitalArtifacts, PhysicalArtifacts, Behaviors, IntelligenceProducts, SecurityConditions, RiskFactors, Countermeasures, and related classes. Aligns with BFO Continuant. The sibling root class CyberActivity covers happenings (Campaigns, Incidents, Operations, etc.)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A technique performed within or against cyberspace, encompassing both offensive and defensive cyber operations." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Cyber Technique" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Mirrors D3FEND's CyberTechnique class. Subclassed by stonework:AttackTechnique (offensive) and stonework:DefendTechnique (defensive), enabling a single property chain to span both ATT&CK and D3FEND individuals." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DataAsset
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DataAsset> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DataAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DataAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A defender-owned collection of data or information with organizational value, such as databases, intellectual property, credentials stores, or PII collections."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DataAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Data Asset" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DataAsset> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Represents data as a targetable resource distinct from the systems that store or process it. Used to model what adversaries seek to access, exfiltrate, or destroy rather than the infrastructure they exploit to reach it."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefendTechnique
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefendTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefendTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberTechnique> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefendTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A prescribed defensive method used to detect, prevent, or respond to adversary behavior within a target environment." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefendTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Defend Technique" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefendTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Aligns with MITRE D3FEND DefensiveTechnique. Framework-specific subclasses (e.g. d3fend:DefensiveTechnique) extend this class when D3FEND data is loaded." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefensiveTactic
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A high-level defensive objective that groups related countermeasure techniques by the protection goal they serve." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Defensive Tactic" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DefensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Aligns with MITRE D3FEND defensive tactics (e.g. Harden, Detect, Isolate, Evict, Restore). Individuals are typically drawn from D3FEND or similar defensive frameworks via the framework mapping layer."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionPlan
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionPlan> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A Step-decomposed sequence of actions for determining whether observed activity constitutes a security-relevant violation, as distinct from the offensive or defensive action plans that respond to one."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Detection Plan" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A specialization of stonework:InvestigationPlan narrowly scoped to violation triage: orchestrating DetectionRule/Signature evaluation and analytic logic to identify whether a violation occurred, typically triggering a defensive Playbook or ControlPlan via a guarded Transition once one is confirmed. Where a Playbook is an action plan carried out by an interested party — offensive (an adversary's operational playbook) or defensive (an incident responder's playbook) — a DetectionPlan plays a neutral, evaluative role, watching for the kind of changes an InvestigationPlan more broadly investigates."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionRule
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionRule> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionRule> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionRule> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured, machine-readable rule designed to detect malicious or suspicious activity, techniques, or indicators. It may be signature-based, behavioral, correlation-based, or analytic."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionRule> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Detection Rule" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionRule> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Encompasses pattern-matching signatures (YARA, Snort, Suricata), behavioral rules (Sigma), SIEM correlation logic, and analytic detections. Signature is a subclass for content-centric, indicator-matching rules."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Any discrete digital object or content entity that can be observed, transmitted, stored, or acted upon in a cyber environment."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Digital Artifact" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes files, hashes, malware samples, network traffic captures, and other content-bearing items. Aligns with D3FEND DigitalArtifact concepts. Sibling of stonework:PhysicalArtifact."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A digital representation or persona of an Agent, inferred from one or more identifiers (email, phone, account, etc.). It may or may not be attributed to a known real-world Agent." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Digital Identity" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Useful when you observe identifiers without confirmed attribution. Can aggregate multiple Identifiers and later be linked to a real-worldAgent."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Directory
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Directory> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Directory> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Directory> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Directory" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Domain
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Domain> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Domain> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Domain> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents a network domain name. Can reference the IP addresses it resolves to." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Domain> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Domain"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailAddress
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailAddress> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents a single email address, optionally associating it with a display name and a user account." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Email Address"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailMessage
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailMessage> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailMessage> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailMessage> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A discrete email communication, including headers, body content, and optional attachments, transmitted over an email protocol."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailMessage> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Email Message"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#EmailMessage> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Distinct from stonework:EmailAddress, which represents the sender/recipient identifier. An EmailMessage is the content artifact itself. Used to represent phishing lures, spear-phishing emails, or other email-borne threat delivery mechanisms. Corresponds to STIX EmailMessage SCO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Event
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Event> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Event> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Event> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An atomic, observable occurrence in a system, network, or environment that has potential security relevance but has not yet been assessed or declared as an Incident."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Event> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Event"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Event> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Represents the pre-triage state — a raw log entry, alert, or sensor observation. Distinguished from Incident by the absence of human or automated assessment declaring it significant and adverse. Useful for SIEM, log-based, and threat hunting use cases."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FalsePositiveRate
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FalsePositiveRate> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FalsePositiveRate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FalsePositiveRate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid260 .
_:genid260 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#falsePositiveRateScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#falsePositiveRateScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid260 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid260 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FalsePositiveRate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An ordinal classification of the expected false-positive frequency of a detection rule."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FalsePositiveRate> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "False Positive Rate"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Feed
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Feed> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Feed> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Feed> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured data source that provides indicators, intelligence, or enrichment data on a regular or continuous basis." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Feed> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Feed" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Feed> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Examples: URLHaus, AlienVault OTX, Abuse.ch, commercial threat feeds, etc."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#File
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#File> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#File> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#File> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A discrete unit of data stored on a filesystem, identified by name, path, and cryptographic hash. Includes executable binaries, scripts, documents, archives, and other file types relevant to cyber threat analysis."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#File> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "File"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#File> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Linked to hash values via stonework:hasHash. A MalwareSample may be associated with its File representation. Framework-specific extensions (PE headers, NTFS streams, archive contents) are modeled at the framework layer."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccount
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccount> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An account that can hold a balance of, or transfer, economic value — fiat currency, cryptocurrency, or monetary-equivalent stored value such as gift-card credit, gaming currency, or loyalty points redeemable for goods."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Financial Account"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Defined by function, not by the holder: an account at a regulated bank, brokerage, or exchange and a stored-value balance at a non-financial merchant are both FinancialAccounts. Whether it is held at a regulated institution is recorded with the optional stonework:heldAt link, not by the class. Account kind (checking, custodial wallet, prepaid card, exchange account) is asserted with stonework:categorizedBy a stonework:FinancialAccountType. Identifiers such as an IBAN, domestic account number, or crypto address attach with stonework:hasIdentifier. Use stonework:accountCurrency for each currency or crypto asset the account holds or is denominated in; it is intentionally multi-valued. Balance over time is a stonework:MetricObservation. A crypto wallet is a FinancialAccount; its on-chain address is a stonework:CryptoAddress."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccountType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccountType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid261 .
_:genid261 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#financialAccountTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#financialAccountTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid261 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid261 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a financial account by the kind of value custody or service it represents."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialAccountType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Financial Account Type"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitution
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitution> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An organization whose business is holding, moving, exchanging, or safeguarding financial assets on behalf of others — a bank, credit union, broker-dealer, payment processor, money services business, or virtual asset service provider."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Financial Institution"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitution> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The natural bearer of institution identifiers (stonework:bic, stonework:abaRoutingNumber) that should not broaden bare stonework:Organization, and the range of stonework:heldAt. Institution kind is asserted with stonework:categorizedBy a stonework:FinancialInstitutionType. The Legal Entity Identifier is a general organization identifier (stonework:lei), not institution-specific."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitutionType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitutionType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitutionType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitutionType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid262 .
_:genid262 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#financialInstitutionTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#financialInstitutionTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid262 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid262 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitutionType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a financial institution by its regulatory form and the service it provides."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialInstitutionType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Financial Institution Type"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialTransaction
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialTransaction> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialTransaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialTransaction> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A movement of economic value from one financial account to another, occurring over a definite period and optionally attributed to the agents responsible for initiating it."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialTransaction> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Financial Transaction"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#FinancialTransaction> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "An occurrent: it starts and settles at a time (stonework:startedAtTime and stonework:endedAtTime), is attributed with stonework:attributedTo, and can be sighted, investigated, and grouped into a stonework:CyberActivityCluster. Originator and beneficiary are given by stonework:originatorAccount and stonework:beneficiaryAccount; correspondent hops by stonework:intermediaryAccount. The transferred value is a stonework:MonetaryAmount via stonework:hasMonetaryValue. Use stonework:originatorMonetaryValue and stonework:beneficiaryMonetaryValue when the debited input and credited output differ, as in a currency exchange. Flat stonework:transactionAmount and stonework:transactionCurrency are convenience projections only for a single-denomination transfer. A blockchain transaction is a FinancialTransaction whose accounts are crypto wallets. Divergent source records of one economic event are handled through stonework:hasProvenance and stonework:Sighting rather than a distinct record class. Use stonework:hasBehavior to link a transaction, or a cluster of them, to a stonework:IllicitFinanceTechnique it exhibits. Use stonework:fundsActivity only when the transaction finances an activity; use stonework:respondsToActivity when a transaction occurs in response to an activity, such as a victim's ransom payment."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#GranularMarking
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#GranularMarking> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#GranularMarking> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#GranularMarking> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A marking application that assigns a data-handling marking to selected fields or portions of another cyber entity."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#GranularMarking> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Granular Marking"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#GranularMarking> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Represents the structural application of a marking, including its selectors and referenced marking definition. It is distinct from a stonework:Markings category value, which expresses the handling designation itself."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Group
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Group> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Group> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Group> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Any collection of Persons, Organizations, or other Groups that share a common purpose or identity but do not necessarily have formal legal structure."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Group> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Group" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Group> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Used for informal collectives, communities, ad-hoc teams, criminal syndicates, or loose alliances. More general than Organization. Can contain other Groups or Organizations."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Grouping
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Grouping> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Grouping> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Grouping> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured intelligence product that asserts shared context among a set of referenced cyber threat intelligence entities."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Grouping> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Grouping"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Grouping> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use when related objects need to be collected without the narrative and publication semantics of a Report. Corresponds to the STIX 2.1 Grouping SDO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hash
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hash> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hash> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hash> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A single cryptographic hash entry associating a hashing algorithm with its hex-encoded digest value. Used to represent entries in the STIX hashes dictionary." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hash> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Hash"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Host
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Host> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Host> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Host> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A computing system, device, or endpoint (physical, virtual, container, or cloud instance) that can run processes, host files, and participate in networks."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Host> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Host"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HumanSource
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HumanSource> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HumanSource> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HumanSource> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A person who provides information, intelligence, or a report (as opposed to automated feeds or technical artifacts)." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HumanSource> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Human Source" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HumanSource> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Often used in HUMINT or OSINT contexts. Reliability scoring is commonly applied to human sources."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hypothesis
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hypothesis> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hypothesis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hypothesis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured, falsifiable prediction about the value a Variable will resolve to once the Behavior or Step declaring that Variable executes."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hypothesis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Hypothesis" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Hypothesis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Formalizes the anticipated side of the Variable lifecycle described in stonework:Variable's scopeNote: a Hypothesis names, in advance, the value an output Variable is predicted to be bound to. Linked to the Variable it predicts via stonework:predictsVariable, and to the prediction itself via stonework:predictedType (a class, the common case — mirrors stonework:guardType), stonework:predictedValue (a specific individual), or stonework:predictedLiteral (a scalar) — mirroring stonework:boundTo/stonework:boundToLiteral on the actual-result side. A Transition's guard properties, which test a Variable's actual bound value, implicitly test agreement with whichever Hypothesis predicted that Variable — confirming it if they match, refuting it otherwise. May attach at any level of a Step/Plan decomposition, since stonework:Step is itself a stonework:Behavior."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HypothesisStatus
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HypothesisStatus> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HypothesisStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HypothesisStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid263 .
_:genid263 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#hypothesisStatusScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#hypothesisStatusScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid263 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid263 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HypothesisStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a Hypothesis's position in the confirm/refute lifecycle."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HypothesisStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Hypothesis Status"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#HypothesisStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applied via stonework:hasHypothesisStatus to Hypothesis individuals. Named individuals: pending (not yet tested), confirmed (actual result matched the prediction), refuted (actual result did not match the prediction)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IBAN
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IBAN> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IBAN> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IBAN> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An International Bank Account Number (ISO 13616): the cross-jurisdiction normalized identifier for a bank account."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IBAN> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "IBAN"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IBAN> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The value is stonework:iban. A single stonework:FinancialAccount may carry both an IBAN and one or more domestic identifiers (stonework:BankAccountNumber) via stonework:hasIdentifier; the IBAN is the normalized form that reconciles differing national account-number formats."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPAddress
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPAddress> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents a single IP address (IPv4 or IPv6)."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "IP Address"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPBlock
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPBlock> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPBlock> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPBlock> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR) block used to represent an IP network range."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IPBlock> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "IP Block"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A first-class representation of a security-relevant identifier value, such as an email address, cryptographic hash, MAC address, phone number, URI, or user-agent string."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Identifier" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Identifier does not provide the RDF identity of every CyberEntity and does not imply that every CyberEntity has an Identifier. Named RDF resources are identified by their IRIs; identifiers originating in external systems are recorded with dcterms:identifier or stonework:externalId. Use the most specific available subclass, such as EmailAddress, Hash, MACAddress, PhoneNumber, URI, or UserAgent. Instantiate Identifier directly only when the identifier type is unknown or no suitable subclass exists."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IllicitFinanceTechnique
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IllicitFinanceTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IllicitFinanceTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IllicitFinanceTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A prescribed method for disguising the origin, ownership, or destination of illicitly obtained funds, or for moving value while evading financial controls."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IllicitFinanceTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Illicit Finance Technique"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IllicitFinanceTechnique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers money-laundering typologies and the adjacent methods of terrorist financing, sanctions evasion, and financial fraud. A neutral structural slot: STONEWORK defines the class but not its individuals. A starter set of FATF-derived typology individuals is supplied by the optional frameworks/fatf.ttl module; other bodies' typology sets can populate the same class. Link an exhibiting stonework:FinancialTransaction or stonework:CyberActivityCluster with stonework:hasBehavior. Because the class sits under stonework:Technique, a concrete laundering chain can later be modeled as a stonework:Procedure that stonework:implements a typology, with no rework."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Impact
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Impact> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Impact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Impact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Impact SHOULD specify the affected asset or mission, the type of consequence, and the severity level. Impact SHOULD be represented using explicit properties rather than encoded in class IRIs or labels." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Impact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The magnitude of harm, loss, or adverse effects that would result if a threat were realized and a compromise occurred, expressed in terms of mission, business, operational, or technical consequences." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Impact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Impact" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Impact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Impact represents the severity of consequences associated with a compromise. It may include effects on confidentiality, integrity, availability, safety, mission success, financial loss, or reputational damage. Impact values may be qualitative or quantitative." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactLevel
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactLevel> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
