<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#impactLevelScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid264 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid264 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An ordinal classification of the magnitude of a security impact."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Impact Level"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid265 .
_:genid265 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#impactTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#impactTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid265 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid265 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of the security dimension or consequence affected by an impact."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ImpactType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Impact Type"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Incident
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Incident> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Incident> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Incident> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A significant adverse event or series of related events that has caused, or has the potential to cause, harm to an organization’s operations, assets, or people."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Incident> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Incident" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Incident> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Distinguished from stonework:Event (a raw, unassessed occurrence) by the presence of human or automated triage declaring significance and adversity. An Incident may aggregate multiple Events, involve one or more Campaigns, and trigger Investigation and Playbook execution."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IncidentStatus
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IncidentStatus> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IncidentStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IncidentStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid266 .
_:genid266 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#incidentStatusScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#incidentStatusScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid266 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid266 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IncidentStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of an incident's position in the response lifecycle."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IncidentStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Incident Status"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Indicator
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Indicator> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Indicator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Indicator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An intelligence assertion that a pattern — atomic, expression-based, or behavioral — when matched in an observed environment, indicates the presence of threat activity attributable to a specific adversary behavior, malware, or campaign."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Indicator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Indicator"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Indicator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The central concept in operational CTI. The term is used by the community in three overlapping senses, all of which stonework:Indicator covers: (1) IOC sense — an atomic observable (IP address, domain, file hash, URL) that has been assessed as malicious or suspicious and tagged for detection; in STONEWORK the underlying observable is the SCO (stonework:IPAddress, stonework:Domain, stonework:Hash), and the Indicator wraps it with the assertion, confidence, and validity window. (2) Pattern sense — a STIX patterning expression over one or more SCOs (e.g., [ipv4-addr:value = ‘198.51.100.0/24’]). (3) Behavioral sense — a multi-step or TTPs-based pattern asserting that an observed sequence of behaviors indicates a technique or campaign. Distinct from stonework:DetectionRule: an Indicator is the intelligence claim (‘look for this’); a DetectionRule is the operational artifact that implements the check (‘here is the Sigma/Snort/YARA rule that looks for it’). Indicators are produced by threat intelligence analysts and consumed by detection pipelines; DetectionRules are deployed in SIEMs, EDRs, and network sensors. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 Indicator SDO. Use stonework:indicates to link an Indicator to the AttackPattern, Malware, Campaign, or ThreatActor it asserts."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Industry
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Industry> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Industry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Industry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid267 .
_:genid267 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#industryScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#industryScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid267 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid267 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Industry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specific branch of economic activity or commercial sector (typically modeled using NAICS or similar codes)."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Industry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Industry"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Industry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Examples: Financial Services, Manufacturing, Retail, Pharmaceuticals."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents systems, software services, or any group of resources used to conduct cyber operations. Infrastructure is used by threat actors and intrusion sets both offensively and defensively." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Infrastructure"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InfrastructureType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InfrastructureType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InfrastructureType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InfrastructureType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid268 .
_:genid268 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#infrastructureTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#infrastructureTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid268 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid268 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InfrastructureType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of infrastructure by its operational role or purpose within a cyber operation."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InfrastructureType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Infrastructure Type"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InfrastructureType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applied via stonework:categorizedBy to Infrastructure individuals. Covers both adversary-controlled infrastructure (C2, staging, phishing) and defender infrastructure (firewall, workstation). Aligns with the STIX 2.1 infrastructure-type open vocabulary."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured artifact produced by an intelligence or analytical process, intended to convey findings, assessments, observations, or sourcing context to support decision-making."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Intelligence Product"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The intelligence product hierarchy covers the outputs of the intelligence lifecycle: Reports, Assessments, Sightings, Notes, and Provenance records. Distinguished from stonework:DigitalArtifact (raw observable technical data) and stonework:Behavior (plan specifications and TTP patterns). Intelligence products are the result of human or automated analytical work applied to raw data — they carry judgment, context, and traceability that raw artifacts do not."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Investigation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Investigation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Investigation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Investigation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A systematic Process of inquiry, evidence collection, analysis, hypothesis testing, and conclusion to understand, attribute, or resolve security incidents, threats, anomalies, or other matters of interest." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Investigation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Investigation" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Investigation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes digital forensics, threat hunting, root cause analysis, and incident investigations. Links to Sightings, Evidence (via categorizedBy), Sensors, and produces Findings or updated Assessments."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An InvestigationPlan is a plan specification; executing one produces an instance of stonework:Investigation."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A Step-decomposed sequence of actions for systematically examining a cyber entity, artifact, or event to determine its nature, extent, or significance."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Investigation Plan" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#InvestigationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The general assessment-plan counterpart to stonework:Playbook on the production side: where a Playbook's execution changes state in the world, an InvestigationPlan's execution (a stonework:Investigation) produces judgment about something that already exists or happened — typically a stonework:Analysis or stonework:Assessment. stonework:DetectionPlan is a specialized subclass, narrowly scoped to determining whether observed activity constitutes a security-relevant violation; broader analytical work (forensic analysis, malware analysis, vulnerability assessment) is modeled directly as InvestigationPlan."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#KillChainPhase
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#KillChainPhase> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#KillChainPhase> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#KillChainPhase> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid269 .
_:genid269 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#killChainPhaseScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#killChainPhaseScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid269 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid269 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#KillChainPhase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A named phase within a kill chain or attack lifecycle framework used to categorize the stage of an offensive or defensive operation."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#KillChainPhase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Kill Chain Phase"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#KillChainPhase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applies to both offensive frameworks (Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain, MITRE ATT&CK) and defensive lifecycle models. Used to tag AttackTechniques, Procedures, or Events with their operational phase."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LaunderingStage
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LaunderingStage> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LaunderingStage> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LaunderingStage> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "One of the classic phases of a money-laundering scheme — placement, layering, or integration — grouping the techniques that serve that phase's objective."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LaunderingStage> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Laundering Stage"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LaunderingStage> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The illicit-finance counterpart of stonework:OffensiveTactic: a high-level objective that groups stonework:IllicitFinanceTechnique individuals via stonework:hasTechnique. A neutral structural slot; the three canonical individuals are supplied by frameworks/fatf.ttl."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Likelihood
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Likelihood> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Likelihood> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Likelihood> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Likelihood may incorporate assumptions, uncertainty, adversary behavior, historical frequency, or environmental conditions. Likelihood SHOULD be represented using explicit properties rather than encoded in class IRIs or labels." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Likelihood> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The estimated probability, plausibility, or frequency with which a threat could be realized and lead to a compromise under specified conditions." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Likelihood> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Likelihood" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Likelihood> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Likelihood reflects the chance that a compromise will occur, considering adversary capability, intent, opportunity, preconditions, and environmental factors. Likelihood values may be qualitative, ordinal, or quantitative." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LikelihoodLevel
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LikelihoodLevel> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LikelihoodLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LikelihoodLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid270 .
_:genid270 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#likelihoodLevelScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#likelihoodLevelScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid270 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid270 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LikelihoodLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An ordinal classification of the assessed likelihood that a security outcome will occur."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#LikelihoodLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Likelihood Level"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents a geographic location. May be as specific as a latitude/longitude coordinate or as broad as a continent. At least one of region, country, or latitude/longitude must be provided." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Location"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "STONEWORK Location is a STIX-style geographic designator, not a BFO Site of occupancy. The BFO adapter maps it to an information content entity that denotes a place."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Log
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Log> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Log> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Log> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured or semi-structured record of events, actions, or state changes generated by a system, application, or network component over time."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Log> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Log"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Log> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Logs are primary data sources for detection, investigation, and threat hunting. Examples include system event logs, authentication logs, DNS query logs, firewall logs, and application logs. Distinct from NetworkTraffic (which captures raw packet data) and from Event (which represents a parsed, security-relevant occurrence derived from log data)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MACAddress
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MACAddress> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MACAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MACAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A media access control address that identifies a network interface at the data-link layer."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MACAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "MAC Address"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MACAddress> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers 48-bit and 64-bit MAC address representations, including values carried by the STIX 2.1 MAC Address SCO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A software object intended to perform malicious functions within, or on behalf of, a threat actor. Encompasses both abstract family-level concepts and concrete specimen-level artifacts."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Malware"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Parent class for stonework:MalwareFamily (STIX is_family=true) and stonework:MalwareSample (STIX is_family=false). Corresponds to the STIX 2.1 malware SDO. Use stonework:categorizedBy to assert behavioral type (e.g., stonework:Ransomware). Use stonework:hasArtifact to link a sample to its captured binary."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareAnalysis
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareAnalysis> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareAnalysis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Analysis> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareAnalysis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured record of findings from static or dynamic analysis performed on a malware specimen or family, including the tool used, analysis configuration, and verdict."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareAnalysis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Malware Analysis"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareAnalysis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Captures the output of a single analysis run: antivirus scan, sandbox execution, reverse-engineering session, etc. Key properties include the analysis product and version, the result verdict (malicious/suspicious/benign/unknown), and references to SCOs observed during analysis. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 MalwareAnalysis SDO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareFamily
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareFamily> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareFamily> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareFamily> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareFamily> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An abstract malware concept representing a named strain, lineage, or campaign of malicious software sharing common behavioral patterns, code ancestry, or infrastructure. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 malware SDO with is_family=true."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareFamily> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Malware Family"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareFamily> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Examples: Cobalt Strike, Mimikatz, WannaCry, BlackCat. A MalwareFamily does not have a specific hash or binary but may be linked to MalwareSample instances via the inverse of stonework:memberOfFamily. Use stonework:categorizedBy to assert behavioral type (e.g., stonework:Ransomware, stonework:Backdoor)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareSample
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareSample> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareSample> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Malware> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareSample> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareSample> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A discrete, observable instance of malicious code — a specific binary, script, or executable artifact that can be identified by cryptographic hash, file characteristics, or behavioral signature."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareSample> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Malware Sample"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareSample> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Represents a concrete sample rather than an abstract family concept. A single MalwareFamily may have thousands of associated samples across versions, packers, and dropper variants. Samples are linked to their family via stonework:memberOfFamily and to their file representation via stonework:hasHash or a direct stonework:File association. Properties such as stonework:drops, stonework:downloads, and stonework:communicatesWith describe the behavior of this specific sample, not the family in general."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid271 .
_:genid271 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#malwareTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#malwareTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid271 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid271 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A behavioral or functional classification of malware, describing what the malware does rather than what it is. Corresponds to the STIX 2.1 malware-type open vocabulary."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Malware Type"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MalwareType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Instances (e.g., stonework:Ransomware, stonework:Trojan) are assigned via stonework:categorizedBy. A malware object may carry multiple types. The skos:notation value gives the STIX 2.1 wire-format string for aspirational round-trip export."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Markings
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Markings> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Markings> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Markings> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid272 .
_:genid272 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#markingScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#markingScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid272 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid272 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Markings> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Defines a data handling marking used to restrict or label how data can be shared or handled, such as TLP markings. Referenced by object_marking_refs or granular_markings."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Markings> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Markings"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricObservation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricObservation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A qualified, time-stamped metric value about a cyber entity, carrying its metric type, source provenance, and optional calculation-model version."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Metric Observation"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use for values that change over time or differ by source, including EPSS probability, KEV membership, confidence, likelihood, risk scores, and normalized monetary valuations. A stonework:metricNormalizedValue observation records its output currency with stonework:valuationCurrency. Direct datatype properties remain convenience projections for consumers that need a single current value."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid273 .
_:genid273 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#metricTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#metricTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid273 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid273 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A controlled classification identifying the meaning and scale of a qualified metric observation."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MetricType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Metric Type"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Countermeasure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/mitigation> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A deliberate action or control intended to reduce the likelihood, impact, or severity of a risk, threat, vulnerability, or adverse event."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Mitigation" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mitigation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Mitigations are proactive or reactive measures applied to address weaknesses or threats. Examples include: applying a security patch, enabling MFA, deploying a firewall rule, isolating a compromised host, or training users. A Mitigation may be a reusable control (ATT&CK sense) or a specific situational action. CourseOfAction is a subclass for contextualized, packaged response actions."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MobileDevice
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MobileDevice> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MobileDevice> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Host> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MobileDevice> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A portable computing device with wireless connectivity, typically used for communication, data processing, and accessing networks (e.g., smartphones, tablets, or laptops with cellular capability)."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MobileDevice> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Mobile Device" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MobileDevice> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "In a cyber threat context, Mobile Devices are often treated as both endpoints (Infrastructure) and vectors for attacks (e.g., mobile malware, phishing, lost/stolen device risks). They may host UserAccounts, run Mobile Applications, and connect via cellular, Wi-Fi, or Bluetooth."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MonetaryAmount
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MonetaryAmount> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MonetaryAmount> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MonetaryAmount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A quantity of money: a decimal magnitude paired with the currency that denominates it."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MonetaryAmount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Monetary Amount"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#MonetaryAmount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Reified so magnitude and currency always travel together, mirroring how stonework:Hash pairs algorithm and digest. Attached to a transaction, account, impact, or demand with stonework:hasMonetaryValue or a monetary-value role subproperty. The magnitude is stonework:amount and the unit is stonework:currency. A currency-normalized valuation of the same amount, for example its USD equivalent at a stated time and rate source, is a stonework:MetricObservation on this amount with stonework:valuationCurrency identifying the output currency."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Motivation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Motivation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Motivation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Motivation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid274 .
_:genid274 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#motivationScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#motivationScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid274 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid274 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Motivation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The reason or driving force behind an adversary's decision to conduct an attack or operation."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Motivation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Motivation"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Motivation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applied via stonework:categorizedBy to ThreatActor, Campaign, or Operation individuals. Named individuals cover the STIX 2.1 attack-motivation open vocabulary. Custom motivations may be added as additional individuals."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mutex
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mutex> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mutex> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mutex> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A named mutual exclusion object (mutex or semaphore) used by a process to prevent concurrent access to a shared resource. Commonly used as a malware indicator — malware families often create uniquely named mutexes to avoid re-infecting a host."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mutex> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Mutex"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Mutex> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Mutex names serve as host-based indicators of compromise. A specific mutex name found on a system can be attributed to a known malware family. Corresponds to the STIX 2.1 Mutex SCO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkService
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkService> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkService> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkService> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A network-accessible service or endpoint provided by infrastructure, such as HTTP, DNS, or SSH servers."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkService> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Network Service" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkTraffic
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkTraffic> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkTraffic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkTraffic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A captured or observed network traffic flow between source and destination endpoints, including protocol, ports, payload characteristics, and timing."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkTraffic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Network Traffic"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#NetworkTraffic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Distinct from stonework:Log (which captures parsed event records) and stonework:Event (a security-relevant occurrence). NetworkTraffic is the raw flow artifact. May carry indicators such as user-agent strings, URI patterns, or certificate fingerprints observable in transit."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Note
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Note> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Note> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Note> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A free-text annotation attached to one or more individuals to provide additional context, analyst commentary, caveats, or confidence assessments."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Note> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Note" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Note> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Notes are analyst-authored rather than machine-generated. They may express uncertainty, sourcing context, or interpretive observations that do not fit structured properties. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 Note SDO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OffensiveTactic
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OffensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OffensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OffensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A high-level adversary objective that groups related attack techniques by the goal they serve during an intrusion or operation." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OffensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Offensive Tactic" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OffensiveTactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Aligns with MITRE ATT&CK tactics (e.g. Initial Access, Execution, Lateral Movement) and D3FEND OffensiveTactic. Individuals are typically drawn from ATT&CK or similar offensive frameworks via the STONES or framework mapping layers."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OnlineReference
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OnlineReference> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OnlineReference> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OnlineReference> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A reference to an external online resource (URL, article, post, etc.) used for provenance." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OnlineReference> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Online Reference" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Operation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Operation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Operation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Operation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A coordinated set of activities conducted by one or more agents toward a defined objective, typically spanning multiple campaigns, incidents, or phases and characterized by unified command, intent, or resourcing."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Operation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Operation" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Operation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Operations are broader in scope than Campaigns — a single Operation may encompass multiple Campaigns executed over an extended period. Applies to both offensive operations (e.g. state-sponsored cyber operations) and defensive operations (e.g. incident response operations, threat hunting operations)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OperationPlan
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OperationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OperationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OperationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A Playbook specifically representing an adversary's operational plan — the offensive counterpart within the Playbook hierarchy."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OperationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Operation Plan" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#OperationPlan> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Named to avoid colliding with the already-established stonework:AttackPattern and stonework:AttackTechnique, and to align with stonework:Operation, whose definition ('a coordinated set of activities... unified command, intent, or resourcing') describes the executed counterpart of an OperationPlan. Generic stonework:Playbook remains available for the undifferentiated or defensive case."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Opinion
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Opinion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Opinion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Opinion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An analyst's epistemological stance on the correctness or credibility of information asserted in another intelligence object."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Opinion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Opinion"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Opinion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Sibling to stonework:Assessment under IntelligenceProduct. Where Assessment evaluates risk or impact, Opinion evaluates the reliability or accuracy of existing intelligence — e.g., 'I disagree with this threat score' or 'I believe this indicator attribution is incorrect'. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 Opinion SDO. The opinion value (strongly-agree to strongly-disagree) and explanation are captured via stones: properties."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Organization> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A formal or semi-formal structured entity such as a company, government agency, non-profit, military unit, or consortium with defined boundaries and usually legal identity."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Organization" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Organization> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Corresponds to STIX Identity with identity_class = 'organization'. Includes corporations, government bodies, NGOs, universities, etc. Distinguished from Group by having more persistent structure, roles, and often legal standing."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PaymentInstrument
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PaymentInstrument> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PaymentInstrument> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PaymentInstrument> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid275 .
_:genid275 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#paymentInstrumentScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#paymentInstrumentScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid275 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid275 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PaymentInstrument> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of the instrument or rail used to move value in a financial transaction."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PaymentInstrument> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Payment Instrument"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Person
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Person> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Person> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Person> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Person> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Person> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A single human individual."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Person> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Person" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Person> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Represents real named people (e.g. employees, threat actors, researchers). Does not include legal entities or groups. Corresponds to STIX Identity with identity_class = 'individual'."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhoneNumber
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhoneNumber> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhoneNumber> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhoneNumber> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A telephone number used to contact or identify an Agent. Used for attribution, OSINT pivoting, and identity clustering."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhoneNumber> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Phone Number"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhysicalArtifact
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhysicalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhysicalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhysicalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A discrete material object relevant to cyber operations — a device, appliance, plant component, or other physical artifact that can be observed, located, or acted upon."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhysicalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Physical Artifact"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#PhysicalArtifact> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Sibling of stonework:DigitalArtifact. Covers ICS and OT assets such as PLCs, RTUs, safety systems, and HMIs, as well as other tangible devices in a cyber-physical environment. An ICS device that runs processes and participates in networks may also be typed as stonework:Host. Distinct from stonework:Infrastructure, which groups resources used to conduct cyber operations."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A Playbook is a plan specification; executing one produces instances of stonework:Process. Playbooks are often versioned and tested before use."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Plan> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A documented, structured Behavior that defines a sequenced set of steps, decisions, and actions to be executed in response to a specific type of event, incident, or operational need."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Playbook" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Playbook> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A Playbook may be offensive (an adversary's operational playbook — see stonework:OperationPlan) or defensive (an incident response, threat hunting, or recovery playbook) — both are action plans carried out by an interested party, as distinct from stonework:DetectionPlan, which plays a neutral, evaluative role. A Playbook decomposes into Steps via stonework:hasStep; its actual execution is recorded as a stonework:Process."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Procedure
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Procedure> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Procedure> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Procedure> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Procedures are instances or patterns of execution that realize a technique. They may include command lines, API calls, malware behaviors, or operator playbook steps. Procedures are distinct from techniques, which describe generalized methods." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Procedure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A concrete, observable sequence of actions, commands, or behaviors that implements a technique in a specific operational context." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Procedure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Procedure" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Procedure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "One realization path for a single Technique — the specific commands, scripts, tools, or configuration that carry it out (e.g. the comsvcs.dll MiniDump way of doing OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory). Technique-scoped and typically linear. A Procedure may be only a prose description — the common case when ingesting MITRE ATT&CK procedure examples — or decomposed into stonework:Step individuals via stonework:hasStep for sub-step depth. Links to the Technique it operationalizes via stonework:implements or its specialization stonework:procedureFor. Distinct from stonework:Playbook / stonework:OperationPlan / stonework:ControlPlan, which are objective-scoped plans that orchestrate several Procedures or Techniques with branching, retry, and control flow; a Plan's Step commonly stonework:implements a Procedure." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Activity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://www.opmw.org/model/p-plan/> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An identifiable activity or sequence of activities performed over time by one or more agents to achieve an operational, security, or business objective. It transforms inputs (artifacts, data, configurations) into outputs and may be governed by plans, controls, or policies." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Process"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Process> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "This class operates at the enterprise/operational level and is distinct from low-level runtime Process objects (e.g., STIX SCO Process). It is the execution-side counterpart to Behavior-side plan specifications such as Playbook, ControlPlan, and ThreatScenario — e.g. executing a Playbook produces a Process instance."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Product represent product families or lines (e.g. 'Apache Log4j', 'Windows 10'). Specific releases are modeled as stonework:VersionedProduct individuals related via stonework:versionOf. CPE entries with wildcard version components map to stonework:Product; entries with concrete versions map to stonework:VersionedProduct."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An abstract software, hardware, or firmware product line identified by vendor and product name, independent of any specific version or release. Represents the collection of all versioned releases built under that product identity."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Product" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "stonework:Product does not carry version, update, edition, or other release-specific attributes. Those belong on stonework:VersionedProduct. The two classes are disjoint."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ComponentContext> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid276 .
_:genid276 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onClass> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> .
_:genid276 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#maxQualifiedCardinality> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger> .
_:genid276 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#installationOf> .
_:genid276 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Represents the most granular product identity level: not what software exists, but where and how it is installed. Multiple ProductInstallation individuals may reference the same VersionedProduct."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specific installed instance of a VersionedProduct on a particular system or environment, as identified by a SWID tag or equivalent installation record."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Product Installation" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductInstallation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "stonework:ProductInstallation is the intended target for SWID tag data. The installing system, path, licensee, and configuration details belong here rather than on stonework:VersionedProduct."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid277 .
_:genid277 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#productTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#productTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid277 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid277 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a product by its primary functional category within a system or architecture."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Product Type"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ProductType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applied via stonework:hasProductType to Product individuals. Aligns with CPE component type designations. A product may have more than one type (e.g. an embedded OS is both firmware and operating-system)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Structured source references used to record the origin and trustworthiness of data and relationships."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Provenance"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes Feeds, Publications, and URL References."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Publication
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Publication> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Publication> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Provenance> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Publication> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured intelligence artifact such as a blog post, advisory, report, or news article." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Publication> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Publication" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A first-class record of an assertion or relationship that may carry provenance, time, and other context not represented by an unqualified binary property or scalar value."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Qualified Assertion"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use assertionSubject, assertionObject, and assertionRelationType for generic directed relationships; subclasses may provide more specific structure. Use direct properties as query-friendly projections when their loss of source, time, or relationship context is acceptable. Provenance is optional in the baseline interoperability profile and required by the strict SHACL overlay."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Region
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Region> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Region> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Location> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Region> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A sub-national territorial division such as a state, province, region, or governorate." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Region> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Region" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Registry
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Registry> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Registry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Registry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Describes elements within the Windows configuration Registry system." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Registry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Registry" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryKey
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryKey> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryKey> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Registry> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryKey> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A named key in a Windows Registry hierarchy that may contain subordinate keys and registry values."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryKey> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Registry Key"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryValue
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryValue> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryValue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Registry> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryValue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A named or default value stored beneath a Windows Registry key, including its value type and literal data."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RegistryValue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Registry Value"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Report
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Report> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Report> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Report> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A collection of threat intelligence focused on one or more topics, such as a description of a threat actor, malware, or attack technique." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Report> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Report" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ResourceLevel
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ResourceLevel> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ResourceLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ResourceLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid278 .
_:genid278 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#resourceLevelScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#resourceLevelScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid278 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid278 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ResourceLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of the organizational resources available to a threat actor."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ResourceLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Resource Level"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Risk
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Risk> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Risk> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Risk> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Risk may reference the threat being considered, the assets affected, the expected impact of compromise, and the likelihood of occurrence. Risk values may be qualitative or quantitative, and SHOULD be represented using explicit properties rather than encoded in class IRIs or labels." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Risk> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A measure of the potential for loss or adverse effects arising from the realization of a threat, expressed as a function of the impact of compromise and the likelihood that the compromise will occur." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Risk> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Risk" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Risk> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "This class represents the assessed or estimated level of exposure to harm for a specific asset, mission, or organization. Risk is typically derived from the combination of impact and likelihood, and may incorporate assumptions, uncertainty, and contextual factors." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A calculated or assessed component used to quantify or characterize security risk, including the potential for harm and the probability of its occurrence."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Risk Factor" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskFactor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes Risk (overall assessed exposure), Impact (magnitude of harm if realized), and Likelihood (probability of occurrence). These are analytical outputs derived from threat and vulnerability data, distinct from the conditions (SecurityCondition) that give rise to them and the countermeasures (Countermeasure) that address them."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskLevel
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskLevel> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid279 .
_:genid279 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#riskLevelScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#riskLevelScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid279 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid279 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An ordinal classification of the assessed magnitude of a security risk."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RiskLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Risk Level"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Role
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Role> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Role> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Role> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A function, capacity, or part played by an entity in a specific context, relationship, or operation." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Role> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Role" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Role> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Roles are typically more stable and relational than Categories. An entity can play multiple Roles. Aligns with patterns in BFO, CCO, and D3FEND (e.g., functional roles of infrastructure or actors)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RoleAssignment
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RoleAssignment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RoleAssignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#QualifiedAssertion> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RoleAssignment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A qualified record assigning a Role to an entity within a particular campaign, operation, organization, or other context."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RoleAssignment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Role Assignment"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RoleAssignment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Create separate assignments when the same entity plays different roles in different contexts or time intervals. The assignment can infer the context-free stonework:playsRole projection through the declared property chain."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RuntimeProcess
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RuntimeProcess> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RuntimeProcess> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalArtifact> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RuntimeProcess> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A running or terminated process instance on a host, identified by PID, executable path, command line, parent process, and associated user account."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RuntimeProcess> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Runtime Process"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#RuntimeProcess> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Represents low-level OS process objects observed during execution — distinct from stonework:Process (enterprise-level workflows). Mutex objects created by a process are linked as stonework:Mutex individuals. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 Process SCO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid280 .
_:genid280 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#sectorScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#sectorScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid280 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid280 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A broad division or category of economic activity, infrastructure, or society used for classification and targeting purposes."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Sector"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sector> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Can represent both commercial industries and critical infrastructure sectors (e.g., from CISA or NIST frameworks)."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberActivity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/#SOSAActuation> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specific cyber activity in which a deployed security mechanism changes the security state of one or more cyber entities."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Security Actuation"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use stonework:madeByActuator for the deployed mechanism, stonework:appliesCountermeasure for the safeguard or procedure being carried out, and stonework:actsOn for each affected entity. Unlike a Countermeasure specification, a SecurityActuation is an occurrence and may carry execution times and produced results. Inspired by SOSA sosa:Actuation without formally importing SOSA. The baseline SHACL profile requires madeByActuator at violation severity — the acting tool's own identity is always self-asserted and known at creation time — but only warns on a missing actsOn: the tooling that performed an actuation typically knows what it acted on in source-native terms (an IP, a hostname, a process ID) before that reference resolves to a canonical CyberEntity individual, the same resolution lag as stonework:RoleAssignment's roleContext or stonework:MetricObservation's metricFor. A SecurityActuation with no actsOn yet is incomplete, not malformed."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuator
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuator> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/#SOSAActuator> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A deployed system, service, appliance, or software agent capable of changing the security state of a cyber environment by applying a countermeasure."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Security Actuator"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityActuator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A SecurityActuator is the mechanism that applies a safeguard, not the safeguard specification itself. Firewalls, EDR agents, access-control services, and patch-management systems are typical examples. A mechanism such as an IPS or EDR agent may be both a stonework:Sensor and a SecurityActuator. Distinct from stonework:Actuator, which is a physical process or plant actuator. Inspired by SOSA sosa:Actuator without formally importing SOSA."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A state, circumstance, or disposition relevant to the security posture of a system, asset, or mission — existing independently of any measurement or assessment."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Security Condition" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Weaknesses. Risk, Impact, and Likelihood are assessed/calculated concepts and are modeled under stonework:RiskFactor."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityObservation
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityObservation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/#SOSAObservation> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A time-bounded record produced by a security sensor or automated tool capturing one or more cyber observables witnessed in a monitored environment, together with frequency metadata."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Security Observation"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityObservation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Machine-produced intelligence: a sensor is not an analyst, but its output feeds the intelligence pipeline in the same way that SIGINT feeds human analysis. Key metadata: the time window the phenomenon was witnessed (first/last observed), how many times it was observed (number_observed), and which cyber observables were captured (hasObservable). Use stonework:madeBySensor to link to the producing stonework:Sensor. Distinct from stonework:Sighting, which records that a known CTI object (indicator, malware, threat actor) was seen; SecurityObservation is the raw sensor-layer record prior to attribution or indicator matching. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 observed-data SDO. Inspired by SOSA sosa:Observation — compatible in concept but does not formally import the SOSA ontology."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sensor
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sensor> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sensor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Infrastructure> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sensor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/#SOSASensor> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sensor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A deployed system, appliance, agent, or network tap that collects data about digital artifacts, network activity, or adversary behavior for detection and investigation purposes."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sensor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Sensor" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sensor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applies DetectionRules via stonework:usesRule. May be a software agent, physical appliance, virtual instance, or network sensor. Examples: IDS/IPS, SIEM collector, endpoint agent, network tap, honeypot, vulnerability scanner. An AI-driven sensor that crosses into autonomous decision-making may be additionally typed as stonework:AIAgent. Aligns with D3FEND Sensor. Inspired by SOSA sosa:Sensor — compatible in concept but does not formally import the SOSA ontology. Produces stonework:SecurityObservation instances via stonework:madeBySensor."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sighting
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sighting> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sighting> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#IntelligenceProduct> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sighting> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Records that a object such as an indicator, malware, tool, or threat actor was seen. Wraps observation metadata around a reference to the sighted object."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Sighting> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Sighting" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Signature
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Signature> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Signature> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DetectionRule> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Signature> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A content-centric detection rule that matches malicious activity by a specific pattern — a byte sequence, string, hash, regular expression, or network packet structure."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Signature> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Signature"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Signature> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes YARA rules, Snort/Suricata rules, and AV signatures. Distinguished from broader DetectionRule subclasses (behavioral, correlation, analytic) by its tight binding to a specific known-bad indicator or artifact pattern."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SoftwareBillOfMaterials
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SoftwareBillOfMaterials> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SoftwareBillOfMaterials> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An SBOM asserts that specific VersionedProduct instances are components of a containing artifact. Component entries are represented as stonework:BomEntry instances linked via stonework:hasBomEntry."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SoftwareBillOfMaterials> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A formal, machine-readable inventory of VersionedProduct components that constitute a software artifact or system, expressed in a standard format such as CycloneDX or SPDX."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SoftwareBillOfMaterials> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Software Bill of Materials" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SoftwareBillOfMaterials> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "SoftwareBillOfMaterials is a native STONEWORK class — not a subclass of any STIX domain object, as STIX 2.1 has no SBOM concept. Instances correspond to SBOM documents; format provenance (CycloneDX 1.6, SPDX 2.3, etc.) is expressed via stonework:bomFormat."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SophisticationLevel
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SophisticationLevel> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SophisticationLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SophisticationLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid281 .
_:genid281 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#sophisticationLevelScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#sophisticationLevelScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid281 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid281 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SophisticationLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a threat actor's assessed technical sophistication."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SophisticationLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Sophistication Level"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Step
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Step> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Step> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Step> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://purl.org/net/p-plan#Step> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Step> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A single, individually addressable unit of action within a Behavior that models a multi-step process, such as a Procedure, Playbook, ThreatScenario, or Control."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Step> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Step" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Step> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A Step is itself a Behavior — a plan specification for a sub-action, not its execution — consistent with p-plan:Step subclassing prov:Plan rather than prov:Activity. Because Step is a Behavior, a Step MAY itself decompose into further Steps via stonework:hasStep, supporting nested sub-procedures (e.g. a Kill Chain phase decomposing into candidate technique attempts) without a separate composite class. The activity that executes a Step is a stonework:CyberActivity; it links back to the Step it realizes via stonework:correspondsToStep."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Tactic SHOULD correspond to high‑level adversary goals. Techniques are typically modeled as children or members of a tactic." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A high‑level objective or strategic goal that groups related techniques by the purpose they serve during an operation." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Tactic" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tactic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Superclass for OffensiveTactic and DefensiveTactic. Tactics represent the strategic intent behind behavior — gaining initial access, exfiltrating data, or hardening a system. They serve as conceptual categories for techniques." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Mirrors D3FEND's Technique class and reflects the PROV-O Plan philosophy: a technique is a plan that describes how an action should be carried out, distinct from the activity that executes it. Subclassed by stonework:CyberTechnique for cyber-specific methods." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A reusable, prescribed method for achieving an objective, which may be instantiated as a concrete execution by an actor or system." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Technique" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Technique> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A method specification, not a plan: a Technique describes an approach ('access credential material in LSASS process memory') without prescribing an ordered, branched sequence of steps. Concrete step structure appears one level down, in a stonework:Procedure that realizes the Technique, or in a stonework:Playbook / stonework:OperationPlan that orchestrates several. An execution links to the Technique it performs directly with stonework:executionOf — no Procedure, Plan, or Step is required for the lightweight case. Technique is intentionally not a subclass of stones:AttackPattern: attack patterns such as CAPEC are abstract taxonomy entries, while Techniques are operationalized methods closer to execution. See stonework:CyberTechnique, stonework:AttackTechnique, stonework:DefendTechnique, and docs/technique-modeling.md." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Threat
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Threat> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Threat> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Threat> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Threat may describe adversary capabilities, strategic objectives, targeting patterns, or environmental conditions that increase the likelihood of harmful action. Threats may be linked to threat actors, campaigns, malware, TTPs, weaknesses, or assets, but are conceptually distinct from the actions or events that realize them." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Threat> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A potential or actual source of harm arising from an adversary's capability, intent, and opportunity to exploit weaknesses or create adverse effects within a system, organization, or mission." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Threat> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Threat" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Threat> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "This class represents the abstract notion of a cyber threat, independent of specific threat actors, campaigns, or events. It provides a semantic foundation for modeling adversary capabilities, intentions, targeting, and conditions that give rise to harmful outcomes." .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Agent> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Agent> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An entity that has the capability and intent to conduct harmful activities within a cyber or sociotechnical context." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Threat Actor"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Parent class for stonework:ThreatActorIndividual (a single human) and stonework:ThreatActorGroup (a collective or organization). Threat actors are distinct from the threats they pose; they are the entities that carry out actions that realize threats. They may be linked to observed behaviors, techniques, targeting patterns, and strategic objectives."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorGroup
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorGroup> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A collective, organization, or coordinated set of individuals operating with common intent to conduct adversarial cyber activities."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Threat Actor Group"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers nation-state units, cybercriminal organizations, hacktivist collectives, and other named adversary groups. Industry names such as APT20 or APT29 are often used for both a tracked body of activity and the presumed group behind it. Model the activity as a separate stonework:CyberActivityCluster and link it to the ThreatActorGroup with stonework:attributedTo when supported. MITRE ATT&CK serializes tracked Groups as STIX intrusion-set objects; that source representation alone does not establish that the activity cluster and responsible group are the same entity. Distinguished from stonework:ThreatActorIndividual, which represents a single human actor."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorIndividual
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorIndividual> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorIndividual> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActor> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorIndividual> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A single, identifiable human actor with the capability and intent to conduct adversarial cyber activities."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorIndividual> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Threat Actor Individual"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorIndividual> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Covers individual hackers, insiders, contractors, and other identified persons. Relevant for law enforcement and prosecution contexts where attribution to a specific individual — rather than a group — is required. Distinguished from stonework:ThreatActorGroup, which represents a collective or organization."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Role> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid282 .
_:genid282 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#threatActorRoleScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#threatActorRoleScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid282 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid282 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A controlled role type describing the function played by a threat actor within a specific operation, campaign, or criminal organization."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Threat Actor Role"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorRole> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A threat actor may play different roles across operations — a nation-state actor may be a sponsor in one campaign and a director in another. Use stonework:RoleAssignment for contextual modeling; stonework:playsRole is the context-free STIX-compatible projection."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid283 .
_:genid283 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#threatActorTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#threatActorTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid283 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid283 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a threat actor by their organizational form, affiliation, or primary mode of operation."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Threat Actor Type"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatActorType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Applied via stonework:categorizedBy to ThreatActor individuals. A threat actor may have multiple types. Aligns with the STIX 2.1 threat-actor-type open vocabulary."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatScenario
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatScenario> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatScenario> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Behavior> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatScenario> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Plan> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatScenario> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A plan specification describing how a threat could materialize against specific assets or missions — incorporating adversary intent, capability, opportunity, preconditions, attack path, and expected consequences."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatScenario> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Threat Scenario"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#ThreatScenario> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "A ThreatScenario is prescriptive and forward-looking: it specifies a potential sequence of adversary behaviors and environmental conditions rather than recording observed events. Used to guide defensive planning, risk assessment, and Playbook development. Distinct from stonework:Incident (an observed occurrence) and stonework:Threat (an abstract source of harm). Aligns with prov:Plan and P-Plan scenario specifications."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tool
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tool> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tool> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Product> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tool> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Legitimate software that can be used offensively by threat actors or defensively by security practitioners. Distinguishable from malware by being designed for benign or dual-use purposes rather than being inherently malicious."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tool> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Tool"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Tool> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Includes commercial penetration testing tools, remote administration utilities, and living-off-the-land binaries (LOLBins) repurposed by adversaries, as well as defender-operated tools such as scanners, EDR agents, and forensic utilities. Corresponds to STIX 2.1 Tool SDO."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#TransactionType
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#TransactionType> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#TransactionType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Category> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#TransactionType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid284 .
_:genid284 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#hasValue> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#transactionTypeScheme> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#transactionTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
_:genid284 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> .
_:genid284 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#TransactionType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A classification of a financial transaction by the kind of movement it represents."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#TransactionType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Transaction Type"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Transition
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Transition> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Transition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A directed edge between two Steps within the same parent Behavior's Step graph, representing progression from one Step to the next."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Transition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Transition" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Transition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Unconditioned, a Transition expresses plain sequence — an alternative to stonework:precededByStep for the same purpose, used when a Step has more than one possible successor and the edges themselves need to be individually referenceable. When a Transition's stonework:toStep targets a Step already reached earlier in the same graph, it expresses a loop back-edge; looping is not a separate construct. Conditional branching is expressed with the guard properties stonework:guardVariable (the Variable tested), stonework:guardType (a class the bound value must be an instance of) or stonework:guardLiteralValue (a scalar it must equal), and stonework:guardOperator (the comparison; omitting it defaults to equality). The Transition fires when its guard holds against the from-Step's bound Variable."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#URI
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#URI> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#URI> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#URI> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents a Uniform Resource Identifier conforming to RFC 3986."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#URI> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "URI"@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAccount
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAccount> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Account> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An authenticated-access identity on a system or service — a local OS account, cloud or directory account, application account, or social media account — through which a principal logs in and acts."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "User Account"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAccount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "The access/authentication sibling of stonework:FinancialAccount under stonework:Account. Where a login governs a value-holding ledger (online banking, an exchange account, a marketplace wallet), model the ledger as a separate stonework:FinancialAccount and link the two with stonework:controls — one login commonly controls several accounts."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAgent
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAgent> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Identifier> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A string identifier transmitted by a client application in HTTP requests declaring the software, version, and platform making the request. Used for fingerprinting clients, identifying malware C2 beaconing patterns, and clustering infrastructure by shared tooling."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "User Agent"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserAgent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "User-agent strings are observable in network traffic, proxy logs, and web server logs. Distinctive or spoofed user-agent values are commonly used as indicators in threat intelligence. Distinct from stonework:UserAccount (an identity credential) — a UserAgent describes software behavior, not a user identity."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserGroup
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserGroup> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#DigitalIdentity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Represents a user group on a system or service, including local OS groups, cloud groups, and social media groups."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#UserGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "User Group" .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Variable
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Variable> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Variable> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#CyberEntity> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Variable> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <http://purl.org/net/p-plan#Variable> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Variable> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A named placeholder for a value declared by a Behavior or Step, bound to an actual value only once the corresponding execution occurs."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Variable> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Variable" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Variable> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Declared via stonework:hasInputVariable or stonework:hasOutputVariable. An input Variable is typically bound before its Step executes from an already-known fact; for example, a plan may bind to an existing stonework:CvssMetric assessment. An output Variable is bound only after its Step executes, such as when an Investigation constructs a stonework:VulnerabilityAssessment. Variables carry execution-time values and do not replace persistent domain relationships. Bound via stonework:boundTo (an individual) or stonework:boundToLiteral (a scalar). The bound value is what stonework:Transition guard properties test, and — in a future deviation-detection layer — what an actual result is compared against what the Step declared as expected."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:VersionedProduct may include operating systems, applications, libraries, devices, or embedded components at a specific release. Versioning, vendor attribution, and edition details SHOULD be represented using explicit properties rather than encoded strings."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specific versioned release or build of a Product, identified by a CPE name or equivalent product identifier. Represents the unit of software actually deployed and potentially affected by vulnerabilities."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Versioned Product" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VersionedProduct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Each CPE 2.3 entry with a concrete version component maps to a stonework:VersionedProduct individual. Wildcard-version CPEs map to stonework:Product. Related to its abstract product line via stonework:versionOf."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Vulnerability
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Vulnerability> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Vulnerability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Vulnerability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A flaw or weakness in software, hardware, firmware, or system architecture that can be exploited by a threat actor to compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Vulnerability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Vulnerability" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Vulnerability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Represents specific exploitable instances (e.g., CVE-identified vulnerabilities) rather than weakness classes — those are modeled as stonework:Weakness. A Vulnerability is linked to its root Weakness via stonework:hasWeakness, to affected products via stonework:affected, to its fix via stonework:fixedInProduct, and to exploiting malware or actors via stonework:exploits. Aligns with D3FEND Vulnerability and NVD CVE."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VulnerabilityAssessment
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VulnerabilityAssessment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VulnerabilityAssessment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Assessment> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VulnerabilityAssessment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A structured evaluation of a specific vulnerability's applicability and risk in a particular product or deployment context."@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VulnerabilityAssessment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Vulnerability Assessment"@en .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#VulnerabilityAssessment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Captures VEX-style assessments: whether a product is affected, not affected, fixed, or under investigation, together with the justification and any remediation statement. Distinct from stonework:Vulnerability (which describes the flaw itself) and stonework:CvssMetric (which gives context-neutral technical severity): a VulnerabilityAssessment is a judgment about applicability and risk in a specific product, deployment, or enterprise context. It may be constructed by an Investigation executing a Plan whose inputs include the vulnerability, its CVSS assessment, and current enterprise graph state."@en .
# 
# https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Weakness
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Weakness> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Weakness> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#SecurityCondition> .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Weakness> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Instances of stonework:Weakness may represent base weaknesses, variant weaknesses, or compound weaknesses. CWE identifiers (e.g., \"CWE‑79\") SHOULD be modeled using explicit properties rather than encoded in the class IRI. Descriptions and extended details SHOULD be referenced via citations rather than reproduced verbatim." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Weakness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A type of flaw, fault, or design condition in software, hardware, or system architecture that can enable vulnerabilities, reduce security assurance, or increase the likelihood of exploitable behavior." .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Weakness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Weakness" .
<https://cyberterrain.org/ns/stonework#Weakness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "This class provides a semantic representation of weakness types defined in the Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE). It supports hierarchical relationships, abstraction levels, and mappings to related attack patterns, vulnerabilities, and security controls." .
# 
